      "math_academy_id": "q-130858",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=14$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(14) \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
